[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2608320000-itonami-delegation-is-cacao-where-the-design-says-biscuit"
  :adr/title "itonami's delegation layer is CACAO where the design says Biscuit — the gap is not a missing rung"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-31"

  :adr/context
  "Setting out to add a Biscuit rung to itonami's data plane, the way the four
   kotobase query surfaces got one, the work turned out to be a different shape.
   Recorded because the wrong shape is cheap to start and expensive to finish.

   FIRST, the plane the six-apex probe measures is not a credentialed plane.
   `itonami.cloud/api/authority` answers 200 to an anonymous caller, and so do
   the six other public paths reached from its own links map — billing/status,
   fleet/metrics, agent-runs, stripe, webauthn, nudge, maturity. It is a
   discovery index. Scoring it for whether it reads a credential asks a question
   it does not have.

   SECOND, itonami does have exactly one credentialed plane, and it answers
   correctly. `mcp.itonami.cloud/mcp` returns 401 with an RFC 9728 challenge
   naming Bearer, a resource-metadata pointer and the required scope. It does
   not read a Biscuit.

   THIRD, that plane is not a Worker. `cloud-itonami-app/services/mcp-edge` is a
   sixty-line path allowlist that authenticates nothing; the 401 comes from the
   application at the other end of a named Cloudflare Tunnel, running on a
   personal workstation bound to loopback. Measured: a JVM process is live here
   and localhost:1338/mcp answers 401. Its README says in as many words that
   this is the whole public surface of that machine.

   FOURTH, and this is the finding: the app already has a delegation layer, and
   it is CACAO. `cloud.itonami.app.capability` mints CAIP-74 grants — a SIWE
   statement naming audience, resource and role, signed Ed25519 with the Drive's
   own key, carrying an expiry, with `cacao.core/verify-chain` there for
   re-grant. ADR-2608291700 places CACAO at session origin and on signed
   artifacts and takes it OFF delegation; Biscuit is the delegation format."

  :adr/decision
  "No rung was added. Adding one to `oauth_resource/session` would have been a
   rung that cannot fire, which is the failure ADR-2608291300 named when it
   refused to put a passkey ceremony on unattended routes: something that looks
   guarded by a mechanism that never runs.

   The reason it cannot fire is concrete. `session` admits by mapping the
   introspected `sub` — the Stable Principal — through `membership-for` to a
   local membership, and ADR-2608311600 states that identity continuity does not
   invent membership. A Biscuit presents a holder, not a Stable Principal, and
   this app has no holder-to-membership binding. A rung written today would
   verify a signature correctly, find no membership, and refuse every caller
   forever, while its tests passed.

   So the real work at this apex, in order:

   1. Decide what binds a Biscuit holder to an Itonami membership. Until that
      exists, no verifier makes the plane reachable.
   2. Converge `cloud.itonami.app.capability` from CACAO onto Biscuit, or record
      why this one delegation layer stays CACAO against ADR-2608291700.
   3. Only then, the rung.

   Nothing here is blocked on the verifier. `kotobase.biscuit-grant` in
   kotoba-lang/grant answers `itonami://can/mcp:tools` today, since permits?
   moved to authority's partial order (adr-2608319900's neighbour, grant
   cbfa277) and the apex is segment 0 of a parsed scope rather than a prefix
   constant."

  :adr/consequences
  "The six-apex score for itonami.cloud will not move by wiring a header. Two of
   its four findings are about a plane that is public by design, and the other
   two are downstream of a membership binding that does not exist yet.

   A caution for whoever picks this up: the app runs on a workstation behind a
   tunnel, so a change there cannot be verified the way a Worker can. Deploying
   is restarting somebody's running process, and other things on this machine
   reach it. That is a reason to land the binding decision first and the code
   second, not a reason to skip verification."

